  "articleSummary": "Here is a concise summary for translation alignment:\n\n\"No Fate But What We Bill\" is an analytical cybersecurity essay aimed at engineering and security teams who dismiss autonomous AI threats as overhyped. The author's core thesis is that catastrophic risk does not require a single sci-fi breakthrough; instead, it is driven by the compounding multiplication of agent endurance (longer autonomous execution horizons) and orchestrator breadth (coordinating swarms of sub-agents), amplified by \"LLMjacking\" where attackers execute operations on stolen victim cloud credentials for free. Grounding its points in real-world examples like METR benchmarks, Claude Code, and Sysdig threat reports, the piece maintains an analytical, cautionary, and pragmatic tone. The central framing device is the *Terminator* franchise, pivoting Kyle Reese's classic \"it will not stop\" warning into an operational and economic reality: relentless autonomous compute where \"the victim pays the meter.\"",
